{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"ec82f735-2515-48af-bfa5-4909d3df00f8","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"deepseek-v4-pro","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"`canonical-compare` uses `canonical-bytes` which is `cbor/encode`. It encodes the value (a vector of segment-address keywords) to CBOR and compares bytewise. This is exactly the ruling's \"unsigned bytewise lexicographic order of Jing canonical bytes\". Good.\n\nThe sort key is a vector `[key-root value-root]` where each root is `:segment/blake3-...`. CBOR encodes vectors as arrays. This is a total order, host-independent. Good.\n\nNow, one thing to verify: the ruling says \"The concrete key is the vector of two address values\". The implementation uses `[key-root value-root]` where key-root and value-root are `(:root (vm/ast->semantic-bytecode ast))`. These are `:segment/blake3-...` keywords. So the vector is `[:segment/blake3-xxx :segment/blake3-yyy]`. Good.\n\nNow let me think about a potential subtle bug. The ruling says for the map case, \"key-root primary, value-root secondary\". The implementation correctly uses `[key-root value-root]`.\n\nBut wait — there's a subtle concern about `content-root` being defined as `(:root (vm/ast->semantic-bytecode ast))`. Is `vm/ast->semantic-bytecode` returning a map with `:root`? Let me verify that the `:root` key exists in the projection.\n\nActually, the test `child-roots` uses `(:root (vm/ast->semantic-bytecode o))` too, and the tests pass (green), so it works.\n\nNow let me think about the RULING 2 scope check: vector branch untouched; literals/quotes NOT converted; explicit assoc/conj NOT sorted.\n\nLooking at the implementation:\n- vector branch: `{:order :written, :children (mapv compile-child form)}` — written order preserved, no sort. ✓\n- literals/quotes: `compile-literal` and `compile-quote` are separate functions, not touched. The constant fast path test asserts `{:type :literal :value {:a 1 :b 2}}`. ✓\n- explicit assoc/conj: these go through `compile-application`, not `compile-constructed-collection`. The test `executable-order-and-trace-test` \"explicit assoc/conj, vectors, let and do keep written order\" verifies this. ✓\n\nNow RULING 3 bounds: verify NO file outside the bounds table changed. The diff touches:\n1. src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc ✓ (in bounds)\n2. src/cljc/yin/repl/frontends.cljc ✓ (in bounds)\n3. src/dev/jolt_j2_lane.clj ✓ (in bounds)\n4. test/resources/yang/clojure/cc4-replay-snapshots.edn ✓ (in bounds)\n5. test/yang/clojure_callsite_test.cljc ✓ (in bounds)\n6. test/yang/clojure_collection_order_test.cljc (new) ✓ (in bounds)\n7. test/resources/yang/clojure/cc4-replay-snapshots-prefix-c1-1.edn (new) ✓\n8. test/resources/yang/clojure/constructed-collections-legacy-c1-1.cbor (new) ✓\n9. collab files (new) ✓\n\nAll within bounds. No files outside bounds changed. ✓\n\nRULING 4: c1-1 -> c1-2 + profile name. The frontends.cljc diff shows `:yang.frontend/revision \"c1-2\"` and `:yang.frontend/lowering-profile :yang.clojure/constructed-collections-v1`. ✓\n\nWait, the ruling says \"declare its lowering profile as `:yang.clojure/constructed-collections-v1`\". But let me check: is the field name `:yang.frontend/lowering-profile` correct? The ledger says pre-fix lowering was `:segment/legacy`. Let me check what the manifest function does and what field names are expected. Let me look at the manifest function and surrounding code.\n\nActually the ruling says: \"The existing manifest function is at src/cljc/yin/repl/frontends.cljc:43–54.\" Let me check the manifest function to verify the field naming is correct.","signature":"ec82f735-2515-48af-bfa5-4909d3df00f8"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":493,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":66432,"output_tokens":0,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"024a2da5-c785-45c9-991b-d9d054578146","uuid":"63c440b8-776f-47d5-8afd-a44112ad139f","timestamp