world-fixorder/src/dev/jolt_j2_lane.clj /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-fixorder/test/resources/yang/clojure/cc4-replay-snapshots.edn /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-fixorder/test/resources/yang/clojure/cc4-replay-snapshots-prefix-c1-1.edn /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-fixorder/test/resources/yang/clojure/constructed-collections-legacy-c1-1.cbor","description":"sha256 of all delivered files","dangerouslyDisableSandbox":true}},{"tool_name":"Bash","tool_use_id":"call_00_wTP9sxf6sMgtRwcnjaVZ0132","tool_input":{"command":"cd /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-fixorder && git show HEAD:test/resources/yang/clojure/cc4-replay-snapshots.edn > /tmp/head-snapshots.edn && python3 -c \"import hashlib; print('HEAD:', hashlib.sha256(open('/tmp/head-snapshots.edn','rb').read()).hexdigest()); print('archived prefix-c1-1:', hashlib.sha256(open('test/resources/yang/clojure/cc4-replay-snapshots-prefix-c1-1.edn','rb').read()).hexdigest())\"","description":"Compare HEAD snapshots vs archived pre-fix bundle"}},{"tool_name":"Bash","tool_use_id":"call_00_ET_C9q2lSWndqpnDqAzdYRS7023","tool_input":{"command":"git -C /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-fixorder show HEAD:test/resources/yang/clojure/cc4-replay-snapshots.edn > /tmp/head-snapshots.edn && python3 -c \"import hashlib; print('HEAD:', hashlib.sha256(open('/tmp/head-snapshots.edn','rb').read()).hexdigest()); print('archived:', hashlib.sha256(open('/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-fixorder/test/resources/yang/clojure/cc4-replay-snapshots-prefix-c1-1.edn','rb').read()).hexdigest())\"","description":"Compare HEAD vs archived pre-fix bundle hash"}},{"tool_name":"Write","tool_use_id":"call_00_ET_XhSAsiPJGQbIRCwYv3Hc3782","tool_input":{"file_path":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-fixorder/collab/yang-clojure/1795030110000-reviewer-fix-constructed-collection-order.deepseek-v4-pro.findings.md","content":"Created-GMT: 2026-11-18 19:41:00 GMT\nCreated-Local: 2026-11-19 02:41:00 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\nCoding-Agent: deepseek\nSession-ID: 024a2da5-c785-45c9-991b-d9d054578146\nRole: Reviewer (compiler provenance)\nReviewed: fix/constructed-collection-order r1 (author glm-5.3), worktree\n  /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-fixorder @ base 706d44f9, UNCOMMITTED.\n  Ruling under review: 1795017757000 (architect, gpt-6-astra).\n\n# Verdict: CHANGES-REQUIRED\n\nOne blocking test-coverage finding (a likely-false \"boundary\" that skips a\nruling-required tie comparison), plus minor notes. Everything else —\nRULING 1 mechanics, RULING 2 scope, RULING 3 bounds, RULING 4 versioning,\nthe re-pin, and the honest-gate — checks out. Details below.\n\n---\n\n## Verified correct (not re-litigated here, recorded for the record)\n\n**RULING 1 mechanics** (`src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:302-372`). The emitter\ncompiles each child exactly once (`compile-child`), computes each child's\nroot via the existing `vm/ast->semantic-bytecode` projection (private\n`content-root`), sorts map pairs by `jing/canonical-compare` over the\ntwo-element vector `[key-root value-root]` (key-root primary, value-root\nsecondary) and set elements by their single root, seeds first, no dedup, no\nre-sort after later passes, metadata kept on each AST. `jing/canonical-compare`\nis the ruling's own comparator (unsigned bytewise CBOR order — confirmed at\n`src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:188`). Correct.\n\n**RULING 2 scope.** Vector branch emits `:order :written` (unsorted); constant\nmaps/sets stay `:literal` (asserted by the \"constant fast path\" checks);\nexplicit `assoc`/`conj`/`let`/`do`/vectors keep written order and their swaps\nstay observable (exercised in `executable-order-and-trace-test`). Correct.\n\n**RULING 3 bounds.** All 5 tracked + 4 untracked changed files sit inside the\nbounds table. No out-of-bounds file was touched (no Jing/CBOR, VM grammar,\nmacro.cljc, semantic lowering, encoder/loader, core prelude, dht.cljc, slice9).\n\n**RULING 4.** `clojure-manifest` advances `c1-1` → `c1-2` and declares\n`:yang.frontend/lowering-profile :yang.clojure/constructed-collections-v1`;\nsupport-profile `:yang.clojure/c1`, `:yang.frontend/spi 1`, grammar, and the\nC1 marker are unchanged. Correct.\n\n**Re-pin.** Archived pre-fix bundle `cc4-replay-snapshots-prefix-c1-1.edn` is\nbyte-identical to the pre-fix active bundle: its SHA-256 (5f28913f…) equals\nboth the claimed r2 pin and `git cat-file` of `HEAD:…/cc4-replay-snapshots.edn`\n(verified independently). Only `:argument-vector` and `:lower-unit` changed\n(root/keys/c1/prov all four digests each); the other six members are\nbyte-identical; `:applications` counts (263/256) are unchanged across the\ntransition. Recorded on the JVM after JVM (46/605 callsite, 10/91,387 order),\nNode (41/516, 10/91,387) and Dart (41 callsite, 10 order, all-pass) agreed.\n\n**Honest-gate.** Jolt refusal is recorded, not bypassed: both\n`fixorder-jolt-*.log` exit 1 at the manifest with the same four UNLISTED\nnamespaces (php-parser-test, code-entry-test, machine-data-test,\nprovenance-test), zero children, and the findings file states Jolt acceptance\nremains open. This matches the ruling's \"zero Jolt children is not a pass\"\nand is correctly reported as a J2-owner dependency, not a green.\n\n**Evidence/log counts vs claims** all match: JVM 46/605, JVM 10/91,387, JVM\ncompiler 116/1,082, Node 41/516, Node 10/91,387, Dart 41/10 all-pass, kondo\n0 errors/1 warning (the warning is the pre-existing `yin.repl.query`\nunused-require at `clojure_callsite_test.cljc:31`, untouched by this diff).\nRED-before-green is genuine: `fixorder-jvm-order-RED.log` records 10 tests /\n91,387 assertions / 91,115 failures before the emitter change.\n\n**SHA pins.** All 8 pre-commit pins in the findings file match the delivered\nfiles (recomputed independently).\n\n**ANTLR note.** The delegate used `clojure -M:antlr-gen` in place of the\npermission-denied `bb gen:antlr` (same generation, different launcher). This\ndoes not affect any recorded result: the JVM compiler/manifest battery\n(116/1,082) passed, so parser classes were available; the Jolt refusal is a\nmanifest-listing refusal, not a parser-availability failure.\n\n**Test-time goldens.** None self-generated: every expectation is pinned\n(`named-arity-operand-roots`, `named-arity-key-order`, `empty-map-seed-root`,\nthe embedded base64 legacy payload) or derived by an independent builder that\nre-projects children through `ast->semantic-bytecode` + `canonical-compare`,\nnever through the emitter's own sort.\n\n---\n\n## Findings\n\n### F1 (blocking) — Family-4 set-element tie is skipped under a false \"unconstructable\" premise\n\n**File:** `test/yang/clojure_collection_order_test.cljc:535-549` (the\n\"identical compiled set elements tie\" block) and the boundary note it encodes.\n\n**Claim under review:** the test asserts a whole-element tie for sets is\nunconstructable because \"every host set constructor available to the form API\n(reader, conj, into, hash-set) collapses [two NaNs]\".\n\n**Why this is contradicted by the same file.** The map-tie block in the *same*\ntest (`order-pairing-multiplicity-oracle-test`, lines ~516-527) builds\n`(assoc {} ##NaN :left ##NaN (list '+ 'n 1))` and asserts `(= 2 (count pairs))`\n— \"both NaN entries survive\". That assertion is green on the JVM (this\nnamespace is one of the 10 passing tests). So in this codebase's runtime two\n`##NaN` forms do **not** collapse: they survive as two distinct keys. Maps and\nsets use the same equality/hash semantics (`=` on doubles is `==`, so\n`(= ##NaN ##NaN)` is `false`, and `Double.valueOf` boxes each literal to a\ndistinct object), so two `##NaN` should survive in a set exactly as they\nsurvive in a map. `(count #{##NaN ##NaN})` is therefore expected to be 2 on the\nJVM and Node, not 1.\n\n**Why the fallback doesn't satisfy the ruling.** The block that is actually\nwritten does not create a tie at all: `#{inner (list '+ 'n 1)}` with\n`inner = (list '+ 'n 0)` contains two *distinct* compiled contents (`(+ n 0)`\nvs `(+ n 1)`), so `element-keys` differ and `ascending?` is trivially strict.\nRULING 3 test 4 requires \"distinct forms compiling to an identical entire\npair/element to exercise ties without occurrence loss\" — the \"pair\" half is\nexercised (map NaN tie, `(= (first pairs) (second pairs))`), but the \"element\"\nhalf is not. The ruling is explicit that \"a newly missing required portable\ncomparison is a failed/incomplete gate, not a green skip.\"\n\n**Disposition required (one of):**\n1. Add the set-element tie: build a host set whose two NaN elements survive\n   (e.g. `(hash-set ##NaN ##NaN)` or `(conj #{} ##NaN ##NaN)`) and assert the\n   compiled `conj` keeps both element occurrences (3 operands: seed + 2) and\n   their `element-keys` are equal — mirroring the map-tie block; or\n2. Provide REPL evidence from each host that `(count #{##NaN ##NaN})` is 1\n   (i.e. that the boundary is real), and re-word the note to cite that evidence\n   rather than an unsupported assertion.\n\nIf (2) turns out true on some host but not others, the set tie must still be\nexercised on every host where it is constructable (JVM/Node), matching the\nruling's per-host requirement.\n\n---\n\n### F2 (minor, informational) — `ascending?` is strict and cannot certify a tie as \"sorted\"\n\n`ascending?` uses `(neg? (jing/canonical-compare a b))`, which is false for\nequal keys, so it cannot be used on the genuine-tie cases. The author handles\nthis correctly by asserting ties with explicit `=` equality instead of\n`ascending?`, and only calls `ascending?` where keys are strictly ordered\n(including the value-root tie-break, whose second elements differ). No defect,\nbut the helper's name over-promises; a `nonneg?`-style \"sorted\" check or a\ndoc note would prevent a future contributor from misusing it on a tie. Not\nblocking.\n\n---\n\n### F3 (minor, informational) — misleading block title\n\nThe \"identical compiled set elements tie without occurrence loss\" block\n(535-549) does not construct a tie; it asserts occurrence preservation for two\ndistinct elements. Rename to reflect what it actually checks once F1 is\nresolved.\n\n---\n\n## Non-blocking notes on honest boundaries (accepted)\n\n- **ClojureDart has no array-map** (`insertion-map` reader-conditional maps\n  `:cljd` to `hash-map`): genuine, documented in-test and in the findings.\n- **NaN-tie maps built via `assoc` rather than a literal**: consistent with the\n  (correct) observation that map/set *literals* and host constructors collapse\n  NaN only under Java `equals`; the `assoc` route is what makes the map tie\n  reachable, and it is genuinely exercised. (This is precisely why F1's set\n  claim stands out as internally inconsistent.)\n- **Metadata-as-content cannot be exercised through two coexisting map keys**\n  (host `=` ignores metadata): the corpus correctly uses distinct values and\n  asserts metadata-as-content across separate compilations. Accepted.\n\n## Conclusion\n\nThe emitter change and the versioning/re-pin/bounds/honest-gate work are\ncorrect and well-evidenced; the one open item is F1 (family-4 set-element tie\ncoverage under a likely-false boundary claim). Blocking on F1's disposition.\n"}},{"tool_name":"Write","tool_use_id":"call_00_UrTq7ibm8UuuQfEkFxER9290","tool_input":{"file_path":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-fixorder/collab/yang-clojure/1795030110000-reviewer-fix-constructed-collection-order.deepseek-v4-pro.findings.md","content":"Created-GMT: 2026-11-18 19:41:00 GMT\nCreated-Local: 2026-11-19 02:41:00 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\nCoding-Agent: deepseek\nSession-ID: 024a2da5-c785-45c9-991b-d9d054578146\nRole: Reviewer (compiler provenance)\nReviewed: fix/constructed-collection-order r1 (author glm-5.3), worktree\n  /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-fixorder @ base 706d44f9, UNCOMMITTED.\n  Ruling under review: 1795017757000 (architect, gpt-6-astra).\n\n# Verdict: CHANGES-REQUIRED\n\nOne blocking test-coverage finding (a likely-false \"boundary\" that skips a\nruling-required tie comparison), plus minor notes. Everything else —\nRULING 1 mechanics, RULING 2 scope, RULING 3 bounds, RULING 4 versioning,\nthe re-pin, and the honest-gate — checks out. Details below.\n\n---\n\n## Verified correct (not re-litigated here, recorded for the record)\n\n**RULING 1 mechanics** (`src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:302-372`). The emitter\ncompiles each child exactly once (`compile-child`), computes each child's\nroot via the existing `vm/ast->semantic-bytecode` projection (private\n`content-root`), sorts map pairs by `jing/canonical-compare` over the\ntwo-element vector `[key-root value-root]` (key-root primary, value-root\nsecondary) and set elements by their single root, seeds first, no dedup, no\nre-sort after later passes, metadata kept on each AST. `jing/canonical-compare`\nis the ruling's own comparator (unsigned bytewise CBOR order — confirmed at\n`src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:188`). Correct.\n\n**RULING 2 scope.** Vector branch emits `:order :written` (unsorted); constant\nmaps/sets stay `:literal` (asserted by the \"constant fast path\" checks);\nexplicit `assoc`/`conj`/`let`/`do`/vectors keep written order and their swaps\nstay observable (exercised in `executable-order-and-trace-test`). Correct.\n\n**RULING 3 bounds.** All 5 tracked + 4 untracked changed files sit inside the\nbounds table. No out-of-bounds file was touched (no Jing/CBOR, VM grammar,\nmacro.cljc, semantic lowering, encoder/loader, core prelude, dht.cljc, slice9).\n\n**RULING 4.** `clojure-manifest` advances `c1-1` → `c1-2` and declares\n`:yang.frontend/lowering-profile :yang.clojure/constructed-collections-v1`;\nsupport-profile `:yang.clojure/c1`, `:yang.frontend/spi 1`, grammar, and the\nC1 marker are unchanged. Correct.\n\n**Re-pin.** Archived pre-fix bundle `cc4-replay-snapshots-prefix-c1-1.edn` is\nbyte-identical to the pre-fix active bundle: its SHA-256 (5f28913f…) equals\nboth the claimed r2 pin and `git cat-file` of `HEAD:…/cc4-replay-snapshots.edn`\n(verified independently). Only `:argument-vector` and `:lower-unit` changed\n(root/keys/c1/prov all four digests each); the other six members are\nbyte-identical; `:applications` counts (263/256) are unchanged across the\ntransition. Recorded on the JVM after JVM (46/605 callsite, 10/91,387 order),\nNode (41/516, 10/91,387) and Dart (41 callsite, 10 order, all-pass) agreed.\n\n**Honest-gate.** Jolt refusal is recorded, not bypassed: both\n`fixorder-jolt-*.log` exit 1 at the manifest with the same four UNLISTED\nnamespaces (php-parser-test, code-entry-test, machine-data-test,\nprovenance-test), zero children, and the findings file states Jolt acceptance\nremains open. This matches the ruling's \"zero Jolt children is not a pass\"\nand is correctly reported as a J2-owner dependency, not a green.\n\n**Evidence/log counts vs claims** all match: JVM 46/605, JVM 10/91,387, JVM\ncompiler 116/1,082, Node 41/516, Node 10/91,387, Dart 41/10 all-pass, kondo\n0 errors/1 warning (the warning is the pre-existing `yin.repl.query`\nunused-require at `clojure_callsite_test.cljc:31`, untouched by this diff).\nRED-before-green is genuine: `fixorder-jvm-order-RED.log` records 10 tests /\n91,387 assertions / 91,115 failures before the emitter change.\n\n**SHA pins.** All 8 pre-commit pins in the findings file match the delivered\nfiles (recomputed independently).\n\n**ANTLR note.** The delegate used `clojure -M:antlr-gen` in place of the\npermission-denied `bb gen:antlr` (same generation, different launcher). This\ndoes not affect any recorded result: the JVM compiler/manifest battery\n(116/1,082) passed, so parser classes were available; the Jolt refusal is a\nmanifest-listing refusal, not a parser-availability failure.\n\n**Test-time goldens.** None self-generated: every expectation is pinned\n(`named-arity-operand-roots`, `named-arity-key-order`, `empty-map-seed-root`,\nthe embedded base64 legacy payload) or derived by an independent builder that\nre-projects children through `ast->semantic-bytecode` + `canonical-compare`,\nnever through the emitter's own sort.\n\n---\n\n## Findings\n\n### F1 (blocking) — Family-4 set-element tie is skipped under a false \"unconstructable\" premise\n\n**File:** `test/yang/clojure_collection_order_test.cljc:535-549` (the\n\"identical compiled set elements tie\" block) and the boundary note it encodes.\n\n**Claim under review:** the test asserts a whole-element tie for sets is\nunconstructable because \"every host set constructor available to the form API\n(reader, conj, into, hash-set) collapses [two NaNs]\".\n\n**Why this is contradicted by the same file.** The map-tie block in the *same*\ntest (`order-pairing-multiplicity-oracle-test`, lines ~516-527) builds\n`(assoc {} ##NaN :left ##NaN (list '+ 'n 1))` and asserts `(= 2 (count pairs))`\n— \"both NaN entries survive\". That assertion is green on the JVM (this\nnamespace is one of the 10 passing tests). So in this codebase's runtime two\n`##NaN` forms do **not** collapse: they survive as two distinct keys. Maps and\nsets use the same equality/hash semantics (`=` on doubles is `==`, so\n`(= ##NaN ##NaN)` is `false`, and `Double.valueOf` boxes each literal to a\ndistinct object), so two `##NaN` should survive in a set exactly as they\nsurvive in a map. `(count #{##NaN ##NaN})` is therefore expected to be 2 on the\nJVM and Node, not 1.\n\n**Why the fallback doesn't satisfy the ruling.** The block that is actually\nwritten does not create a tie at all: `#{inner (list '+ 'n 1)}` with\n`inner = (list '+ 'n 0)` contains two *distinct* compiled contents (`(+ n 0)`\nvs `(+ n 1)`), so `element-keys` differ and `ascending?` is trivially strict.\nRULING 3 test 4 requires \"distinct forms compiling to an identical entire\npair/element to exercise ties without occurrence loss\" — the \"pair\" half is\nexercised (map NaN tie, `(= (first pairs) (second pairs))`), but the \"element\"\nhalf is not. The ruling is explicit that \"a newly missing required portable\ncomparison is a failed/incomplete gate, not a green skip.\"\n\n**Disposition required