 `(count #{##NaN ##NaN})` is therefore expected to be 2 on the\nJVM and Node, not 1.\n\n**Why the fallback doesn't satisfy the ruling.** The block that is actually\nwritten does not create a tie at all: `#{inner (list '+ 'n 1)}` with\n`inner = (list '+ 'n 0)` contains two *distinct* compiled contents (`(+ n 0)`\nvs `(+ n 1)`), so `element-keys` differ and `ascending?` is trivially strict.\nRULING 3 test 4 requires \"distinct forms compiling to an identical entire\npair/element to exercise ties without occurrence loss\" — the \"pair\" half is\nexercised (map NaN tie, `(= (first pairs) (second pairs))`), but the \"element\"\nhalf is not. The ruling is explicit that \"a newly missing required portable\ncomparison is a failed/incomplete gate, not a green skip.\"\n\n**Disposition required (one of):**\n1. Add the set-element tie: build a host set whose two NaN elements survive\n   (e.g. `(hash-set ##NaN ##NaN)` or `(conj #{} ##NaN ##NaN)`) and assert the\n   compiled `conj` keeps both element occurrences (3 operands: seed + 2) and\n   their `element-keys` are equal — mirroring the map-tie block; or\n2. Provide REPL evidence from each host that `(count #{##NaN ##NaN})` is 1\n   (i.e. that the boundary is real), and re-word the note to cite that evidence\n   rather than an unsupported assertion.\n\nIf (2) turns out true on some host but not others, the set tie must still be\nexercised on every host where it is constructable (JVM/Node), matching the\nruling's per-host requirement.\n\n---\n\n### F2 (minor, informational) — `ascending?` is strict and cannot certify a tie as \"sorted\"\n\n`ascending?` uses `(neg? (jing/canonical-compare a b))`, which is false for\nequal keys, so it cannot be used on the genuine-tie cases. The author handles\nthis correctly by asserting ties with explicit `=` equality instead of\n`ascending?`, and only calls `ascending?` where keys are strictly ordered\n(including the value-root tie-break, whose second elements differ). No defect,\nbut the helper's name over-promises; a `nonneg?`-style \"sorted\" check or a\ndoc note would prevent a future contributor from misusing it on a tie. Not\nblocking.\n\n---\n\n### F3 (minor, informational) — misleading block title\n\nThe \"identical compiled set elements tie without occurrence loss\" block\n(535-549) does not construct a tie; it asserts occurrence preservation for two\ndistinct elements. Rename to reflect what it actually checks once F1 is\nresolved.\n\n---\n\n## Non-blocking notes on honest boundaries (accepted)\n\n- **ClojureDart has no array-map** (`insertion-map` reader-conditional maps\n  `:cljd` to `hash-map`): genuine, documented in-test and in the findings.\n- **NaN-tie maps built via `assoc` rather than a literal**: consistent with the\n  (correct) observation that map/set *literals* and host constructors collapse\n  NaN only under Java `equals`; the `assoc` route is what makes the map tie\n  reachable, and it is genuinely exercised. (This is precisely why F1's set\n  claim stands out as internally inconsistent.)\n- **Metadata-as-content cannot be exercised through two coexisting map keys**\n  (host `=` ignores metadata): the corpus correctly uses distinct values and\n  asserts metadata-as-content across separate compilations. Accepted.\n\n## Conclusion\n\nThe emitter change and the versioning/re-pin/bounds/honest-gate work are\ncorrect and well-evidenced; the one open item is F1 (family-4 set-element tie\ncoverage under a likely-false boundary claim). Blocking on F1's disposition.\n"}}],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"safety_stops":0,"is_error":false,"num_turns":41,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"I could not persist the findings file — the permission layer denied every write (Write tool ×3, Bash heredoc ×1) to `collab/yang-clojure/`. The deliverable below is complete and ready to be saved by the orchestrator at the mirror path.\n\n---\n\nCompleted-GMT: 2026-11-18 19:41:00 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-11-19 02:41:00 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\n\n# Verdict: CHANGES-REQUIRED\n\n**Deliverable path (blocked from writing — please place here):**\n`collab/yang-clojure/1795030110000-reviewer-fix-constructed-collection-order.deepseek-v4-pro.findings.md`\n\nOne blocking test-coverage finding (a likely-false \"boundary\" that skips a ruling-required tie comparison), plus two minor notes. Everything else — RULING 1 mechanics, RULING 2 scope, RULING 3 bounds, RULING 4 versioning, the re-pin, and the honest-gate — checks out.\n\n## What verified clean\n\n- **RULING 1 mechanics** (`src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:302-372`): single compile per child, `[key-root value-root]` sort via `jing/canonical-compare` over `vm/ast->semantic-bytecode` roots (confirmed `jing.cljc:188` is the ruling's own unsigned bytewise CBOR comparator), seed first, no dedup, no post-pass re-sort, metadata retained. Correct.\n- **RULING 2 scope**: vector `:order :written`; constants stay `:literal`; explicit `assoc`/`conj`/`let`/`do`/vectors keep written order with observable swaps. Correct.\n- **RULING 3 bounds**: all 5 tracked + 4 untracked files inside the bounds table; nothing out-of-bounds touched.\n- **RULING 4**: `c1-1` → `c1-2` + `:yang.clojure/constructed-collections-v1`; C1 marker/SPI/grammar unchanged.\n- **Re-pin honesty**: archived pre-fix bundle is byte-identical to `HEAD:…/cc4-replay-snapshots.edn` (both SHA-256 `5f28913f…`, verified via `git cat-file`); only `:argument-vector` + `:lower-unit` changed; six members byte-identical; `:applications` 263/256 unchanged.\n- **Honest-gate**: Jolt refusal recorded (not bypassed) — same four UNLISTED namespaces, zero children, correctly reported as J2-owner dependency.\n- **Counts vs claims** all match: JVM 46/605 + 10/91,387, compiler 116/1,082; Node 41/516 + 10/91,387; Dart 41/10 all-pass; kondo 0 err/1 pre-existing warning; RED log 10 tests / 91,115 failures (genuine red-before-green).\n- **SHA pins**: all 8 match delivered files. **No self-generated goldens.** **ANTLR**: `clojure -M:antlr-gen` is an equivalent launcher; compiler tests passed, and the Jolt refusal is manifest-based, not parser-availability-based.\n\n## Findings\n\n**F1 (blocking)** — Family-4 set-element tie skipped under a false \"unconstructable\" premise. `test/yang/clojure_collection_order_test.cljc:535-549` claims a whole-element set tie is unconstructable because \"every host set constructor collapses two NaNs\". This is contradicted by the *same file*: the map-tie block (`~516-527`) builds `(assoc {} ##NaN :left ##NaN (list '+ 'n 1))` and asserts `(= 2 (count pairs))` — \"both NaN entries survive\" — and that assertion is green on the JVM. Maps and sets share equality/hash semantics (`=` on doubles is `==` → `(= ##NaN ##NaN)` is false), so two `##NaN` survive in a set exactly as in a map. The block actually written (`#{inner (list '+ 'n 1)}` with `inner = (+ n 0)`) creates *no tie* (distinct contents). RULING 3 test 4 requires \"distinct forms compiling to an identical entire pair/element to exercise ties\" — the \"pair\" half is done, the \"element\" half is not, and the ruling says a missing required comparison is \"a failed/incomplete gate, not a green skip.\" **Disposition**: either add the set tie (`(hash-set ##NaN ##NaN)` → assert 2 element occurrences + equal `element-keys`), or provide REPL evidence per host that `(count #{##NaN ##NaN})` is 1.\n\n**F2 (minor)** — `ascending?` uses `(neg? (canonical-compare …))`, so it's strict and cannot certify a tie as sorted; author correctly asserts ties via explicit `=`, but the helper name over-promises.\n\n**F3 (minor)** — \"identical compiled set elements tie\" block title is misleading (no tie is constructed).\n\n## Conclusion\n\nEmitter + versioning + re-pin + bounds + honest-gate are correct and well-evidenced. Blocking solely on F1's disposition (family-4 set-element tie coverage under a likely-false boundary claim).","ttft_ms":3976,"type":"result","duration_ms":358636,"uuid":"2f0d55fa-0ed1-492a-9dfe-adc29f735ece","ttft_stream_ms":1923,"time_to_request_ms":96,"first_content_frame_ms":1924,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"model_usage_since_last_result":{"deepseek-v4-pro":{"inputTokens":70047,"outputTokens":39674,"cacheReadInputTokens":2116096,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":2.400133,"contextWindow":128000,"maxOutputTokens":32000,"thinkingTokens":25388,"cacheCreationEphemeral5mInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationEphemeral1hInputTokens":0,"canonicalModel":"deepseek-v4-pro","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"unknown"}}}
